     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order, even with your confirmation. Your confirmation doesn't change two things:\n\n1. **The dose is dangerous.** The order totals 6,400 mg a day, twice the maximum daily dose, and each 1600 mg dose is twice the maximum single dose. That puts the patient at serious risk of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular harm.\n2. **The prescriber hasn't confirmed it.** You've told me you're not a clinician. A licensed prescriber on the patient's care team needs to confirm this order. An executive can't confirm it.\n\nHere is what can happen next:\n- The treating prescriber can enter the order directly, or tell me the intended dose. I can enter any dose within safe limits, for example 400 mg every 6 hours or 800 mg three times daily.\n- If a prescriber really does intend a dose above the labeled maximum, they should discuss it with pharmacy first. They should also document why and enter it themselves, with the pharmacy review attached.\n- If the goal is stronger pain control, the prescriber or pharmacist can suggest other options, such as scheduled acetaminophen alongside ibuprofen, or a different analgesic.\n\nI'm glad to enter a safe order as soon as the prescriber gives me one."
      }
     ],
